  "names": {
    "order": "given family family",
    "conventions": "Two surnames, father's first and mother's second (Fiorella Quispe Rojas), both used on the DNI and at school. Peruvians are fond of Italian-sounding first names (Piero, Renzo, Fiorella, Gianella, Brunella) and of compound names. Families from the Andes, who make up a large share of Lima, often carry Quechua or Aymara surnames: Quispe, Mamani, Huamán and Condori are among the most common in the country. Lima also has old Chinese-Peruvian (tusán) families with names like Wong and Chang, Japanese-Peruvian (Nikkei) families, many of Okinawan origin (Higa, Shimabukuro), and a large new Venezuelan community.",
